{"term":"putear","lang":"es","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"insults","subcategory":"slut_shame","concept":"slur_derived_mistreatment","is_novel_concept":true,"register":"slang_crude","crude":70,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":88,"recognition_pct_peak":88,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"To mistreat, harass, or make life difficult for someone; derived from 'puta' (whore), with semantic drift from sex-slur verb to general abuse verb; also means to work as a prostitute in some dialects.","english_term":"to fuck with (someone)","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Polysemous: (1) to give someone a hard time/mistreat them [most common, pan-Hispanic], (2) to hurl whore-slurs at someone, (3) to engage in sex work [regional, less common]. The mistreatment sense has fully decoupled from its sexual origin in everyday use."}
{"term":"jodido/a","lang":"es","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"insults","subcategory":"fuck_derived_insult","concept":"fucker_insult","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":68,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":98,"recognition_pct_peak":98,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Fucked; screwed; ruined; also used as an intensifying adjective meaning 'damned' or 'bloody'; derived from 'joder' (to fuck).","english_term":"fucked","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Extremely common pan-Hispanic expletive adjective. 'Estoy jodido' = I'm screwed. 'El jodido coche' = the damned car. Crude rating is lower than English 'fucked' because high frequency of use has partially neutralized its shock value across Spain and Latin America."}
{"term":"sinvergüenza","lang":"es","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"slut_shame","concept":"sexual_permissiveness_pejorative","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":38,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":98,"recognition_pct_peak":98,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Shameless person; a scoundrel or brazenly immoral individual who acts without sexual or social restraint; literally 'without shame' (sin = without, vergüenza = shame).","english_term":"shameless person","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"In honor-shame cultures, 'vergüenza' carries strong sexual-moral coding, especially for women; calling a woman 'sinvergüenza' implies sexual and moral abandonment simultaneously. For men it more often implies general roguishness. Gender-neutral noun but gendered in its connotations."}
{"term":"cabroncete","lang":"es","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"insults","subcategory":"cuckoldry","concept":"cuckoldry","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":52,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":72,"recognition_pct_peak":72,"peak_year":null,"regional":"ES","definition":"Diminutive of 'cabrón' (etymologically: cuckold; literally: male goat); used as a mild insult or sometimes affectionate address for a minor rogue or scoundrel; reduces the severity of 'cabrón'.","english_term":"little bastard","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Diminutive suffix '-ete' softens the insult; can be used affectionately between close friends. Etymologically from 'cabra' (goat) → 'cabrón' (big goat / man whose wife sleeps around). More characteristic of Spain than Latin America, where 'cabrón' itself is used more."}
{"term":"gonorrea","lang":"es","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"insults","subcategory":"disease_insult","concept":"bastard_insult","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":83,"technical":12,"recognition_pct_current":82,"recognition_pct_peak":82,"peak_year":null,"regional":"CO","definition":"Literally gonorrhea (the STI); repurposed in Colombian and Venezuelan slang as a strong pejorative meaning bastard, asshole, or contemptible person; one of the strongest everyday insults in Colombian Spanish.","english_term":"bastard","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Classic disease-name-as-insult pattern. In Colombia 'gonorrea' as an insult is highly context-dependent: between close friends it can be warmly provocative; directed at a stranger it is a serious threat. Literal STI meaning would score crude≈10, technical≈80; the insult meaning is scored here."}
{"term":"hp","lang":"es","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"insults","subcategory":"maternal_insult","concept":"son_of_a_bitch_insult","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":90,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":90,"recognition_pct_peak":90,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Abbreviation of 'hijo de puta' (son of a whore); a very strong general-purpose insult and exclamation, considered harsher than its English equivalent 'son of a bitch' because 'puta' is sexually explicit rather than canine.","english_term":"son of a bitch","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Written abbreviation used especially in digital communication to soften the visual impact while preserving the referent. 'Hijo de puta' is one of the most fundamental Spanish curses; the 'whore-mother' framing makes it more sexually charged than the English parallel."}
{"term":"culicagado","lang":"es","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"insults","subcategory":"coward_insult","concept":"pussy_coward","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":74,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":58,"recognition_pct_peak":58,"peak_year":null,"regional":"CO","definition":"Coward; literally 'shit-ass' (culo = ass, cagado = shit-soiled/scared stiff); someone so frightened they have defecated in their pants; also a general pejorative for a weak or contemptible person.","english_term":"chickenshit","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Compound of 'culo' (ass) and 'cagado' (covered in shit / scared); primarily Colombian slang. The 'scared-into-shitting' etymology is transparent and drives the cowardice meaning. Closely related to 'cagado de miedo' (shit-scared) used across Spanish."}
{"term":"culiao","lang":"es","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"insults","subcategory":"anal_sex_derived_insult","concept":"fucker_insult","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":87,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":70,"recognition_pct_peak":70,"peak_year":null,"regional":"CL","definition":"Chilean all-purpose crude insult and intensifier derived from 'culear' (to have anal sex); the single most characteristic Chilean expletive, equivalent to 'fucker' or 'motherfucker' depending on context.","english_term":"fucker","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Past participle of 'culear'; literally 'the one who was anally fucked.' In Chile, used constantly as an intensifier between friends with low offense in-group, or as a harsh insult toward strangers. Spelling variant of 'culiado.' Virtually synonymous with 'huevón' in casual Chilean speech."}
{"term":"huevón/weón","lang":"es","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"insults","subcategory":"testicle_derived_insult","concept":"dickhead_insult","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":52,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":96,"recognition_pct_peak":96,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'big-balled person'; used either as a friendly term of address meaning 'dude/bro' (especially in Chile) or as an insult meaning idiot/dumbass, from the folk notion that oversized testicles impede rational thought.","english_term":"dumbass","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"One of the most context-dependent words in Spanish: in Chile 'huevón' between friends is warm and unmarked (like 'dude'), but directed at a stranger it is a strong insult. 'Weón' is the informal orthographic variant. Understood across Latin America; most characteristic of Chile and Colombia. Crude score reflects the mid-range between affectionate and insulting uses."}
{"term":"conchasumadre","lang":"es","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"insults","subcategory":"maternal_genital_insult","concept":"motherfucker_insult","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":96,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":62,"recognition_pct_peak":62,"peak_year":null,"regional":"CL","definition":"Literally 'your mother's cunt/vagina'; a very strong compound expletive invoking the mother's genitalia as the ultimate insult; the strongest common Andean Spanish expletive, analogous in force to English 'motherfucker'.","english_term":"motherfucker","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"'Concha' = vagina in Chilean/Rioplatense slang (elsewhere 'concha' = seashell, causing unintended comedy in pan-Hispanic contexts). Considered more taboo than 'hijo de puta' in Chilean Spanish due to the direct anatomical genital reference. Also used in Argentina and other Andean countries."}
{"term":"prostituta","lang":"es","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"sex_work_pornography","subcategory":"sex_worker","concept":"sex_worker","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"clinical","crude":20,"technical":65,"recognition_pct_current":99,"recognition_pct_peak":99,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Prostitute; the standard formal and clinical Spanish term for a woman who provides sexual services for payment.","english_term":"prostitute","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Default formal register term in legal, journalistic, and medical contexts. Not a slur but carries social stigma. Masculine form 'prostituto' exists but is rarely used. Preferred by institutions over more colloquial or stigmatizing alternatives."}
{"term":"prostitución","lang":"es","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"sex_work_pornography","subcategory":"sex_work","concept":"sex_work","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"clinical","crude":15,"technical":72,"recognition_pct_current":99,"recognition_pct_peak":99,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Prostitution; the practice or trade of engaging in sexual activity in exchange for payment; the standard institutional term for the sex work industry.","english_term":"prostitution","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Near-cognate to English 'prostitution.' Used in law, journalism, policy, and academia. Carries moral and legal associations but is not itself crude. Favored in conservative and institutional discourse over the rights-based 'trabajo sexual'."}
{"term":"meretriz","lang":"es","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":"sex_worker_archaic","concept":"sex_worker_archaic","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":25,"technical":62,"recognition_pct_current":43,"recognition_pct_peak":65,"peak_year":1900,"regional":null,"definition":"Prostitute; an archaic and literary term for a woman who sells sexual services, derived directly from Latin 'meretrix'; used in elevated prose, historical writing, and ecclesiastical texts.","english_term":"harlot","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"From Latin 'meretrix' (prostitute, from 'merere' = to earn/deserve). Appears in Spanish Golden Age literature and colonial religious texts. Rarely used in contemporary speech; recognized mainly by educated speakers. The elevated register strips it of visceral crudeness despite its referent."}
{"term":"ramera","lang":"es","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":"sex_worker_archaic","concept":"sex_worker_archaic","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":48,"technical":18,"recognition_pct_current":55,"recognition_pct_peak":72,"peak_year":1600,"regional":null,"definition":"Prostitute; an archaic Spanish term derived from 'rama' (branch), referencing the practice of hanging a branch at the entrance of a brothel as a commercial sign in medieval Spain.","english_term":"strumpet","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Historical etymology: medieval Spanish brothels and prostitutes displayed a 'rama' (branch) as a trade sign. Largely displaced by 'prostituta' and 'puta' in modern speech; recognized by most Spanish speakers but actively used only in historical, literary, or humorous contexts. More informal and pejorative in register than 'meretriz'."}
{"term":"furcia","lang":"es","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"sex_work_pornography","subcategory":"slut_shame","concept":"promiscuous_woman_slur","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":68,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":62,"recognition_pct_peak":76,"peak_year":1985,"regional":"ES","definition":"Slang for a prostitute or sexually promiscuous woman; a pejorative slur used primarily in Spain that straddles actual sex work and general sexual shaming of women perceived as loose.","english_term":"slut","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Primarily Iberian Spanish; less common in Latin America. Can denote a literal sex worker or any woman deemed sexually promiscuous. Etymology uncertain; possibly from Italian 'forca' or a Germanic source. Has declined somewhat since the 1980s–90s peak but still in active use."}
{"term":"puta","lang":"es","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"sex_work_pornography","subcategory":"slut_shame","concept":"slut_shame","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":90,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":99,"recognition_pct_peak":99,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Whore; prostitute; also the foundational Spanish expletive and all-purpose insult for women; the source root of virtually every Spanish sexual slur in this section.","english_term":"whore","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Functions as: (1) noun for a sex worker, (2) sexual insult directed at any woman, (3) standalone expletive ('¡Puta!' = Fuck!), (4) intensifying modifier ('de puta madre' = fucking great). The single most generative word in Spanish sexual vocabulary; nearly every insult in the preceding section derives from it."}
{"term":"trabajo sexual","lang":"es","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"sex_work_pornography","subcategory":"sex_work","concept":"sex_work","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"clinical","crude":5,"technical":68,"recognition_pct_current":74,"recognition_pct_peak":74,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Sex work; the modern rights-affirming term for the provision of sexual services for payment; direct calque of the English advocacy term.","english_term":"sex work","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Reflects a rights-based framework adopted by advocacy organizations and progressive media since the 1990s. Less common among older speakers and absent from conservative institutional discourse, which prefers 'prostitución'. Recognition lower among older speakers; higher among urban and educated demographics."}
{"term":"trabajadora sexual","lang":"es","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"sex_work_pornography","subcategory":"sex_worker","concept":"sex_worker","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"clinical","crude":5,"technical":68,"recognition_pct_current":70,"recognition_pct_peak":70,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Sex worker (feminine); the modern, rights-affirming Spanish term for a woman who engages in sex work professionally; direct calque of the English advocacy term.","english_term":"sex worker","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Preferred by sex worker organizations (e.g. AMMAR in Argentina, OTRAS in Spain) and in progressive journalism. Masculine form 'trabajador sexual' exists but is less commonly used. Framing sex work as labor ('trabajo') is itself a political stance contested in conservative and abolitionist discourse."}
{"term":"chica de alterne","lang":"es","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"sex_work_pornography","subcategory":"hostess_sex_worker","concept":"sex_worker","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"euphemism","crude":22,"technical":8,"recognition_pct_current":68,"recognition_pct_peak":82,"peak_year":1992,"regional":"ES","definition":"Hostess or bar girl who works in a Spanish club encouraging male customers to buy drinks, typically in a context that is a legal cover for sex work; the standard Spanish euphemism for a woman working in the 'alterne' sex industry.","english_term":"bar girl","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"'Alterne' refers to the practice of a woman circulating among male bar customers to boost drink sales, with sex work implied or explicit but legally deniable. Characteristic of Spanish 'clubs de alterne' which operate as brothels while nominally being entertainment venues. Declined since its 1980s–90s peak as the industry shifted online."}
{"term":"dama de compañía","lang":"es","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"sex_work_pornography","subcategory":"courtesan","concept":"courtesan","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"euphemism","crude":10,"technical":8,"recognition_pct_current":82,"recognition_pct_peak":82,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Companion lady; a polished euphemism for an upscale escort or high-end sex worker, foregrounding social companionship while leaving the sexual transaction unstated.","english_term":"companion","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Classic euphemistic register: the sexual element is linguistically erased, replaced by 'companionship' ('compañía'). Widely used in newspaper classifieds and escort directories. Parallels English 'escort' or 'companion' in its coded meaning; implies higher social status and discretion than 'prostituta'."}
{"term":"escort","lang":"es","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"sex_work_pornography","subcategory":"high_end_sex_worker","concept":"sex_worker","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"loanword","crude":25,"technical":15,"recognition_pct_current":87,"recognition_pct_peak":87,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Escort; a high-end sex worker or paid companion, borrowed directly from English; connotes greater discretion and higher social register than 'prostituta'.","english_term":"escort","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"English loanword used across the Spanish-speaking world; the English word's connotation of upscale, discreet sex work has transferred intact into Spanish. Standard in online classified advertising and escort directories. More neutral in affect than any native Spanish equivalent."}
{"term":"yira","lang":"es","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"sex_work_pornography","subcategory":"street_prostitution","concept":"street_prostitution","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"dated_slang","crude":62,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":33,"recognition_pct_peak":70,"peak_year":1940,"regional":"AR","definition":"Argentine Rioplatense slang for a street prostitute; from 'girar' (to walk around / to turn), evoking a woman who walks the streets soliciting clients.","english_term":"streetwalker","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Classic lunfardo (Buenos Aires criminal argot) vocabulary, now dated. The related verb 'yirar' (to walk the streets as a sex worker) also exists. Immortalized in Enrique Santos Discépolo's 1929 tango 'Yira, yira.' Recognition mostly confined to older Rioplatense speakers and tango culture enthusiasts today."}
{"term":"burdel","lang":"es","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"sex_work_pornography","subcategory":"brothel","concept":"brothel","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":34,"technical":28,"recognition_pct_current":92,"recognition_pct_peak":92,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Brothel; the standard colloquial Spanish word for an establishment where clients pay for sex; slightly informal compared to 'prostíbulo' but the more common everyday term.","english_term":"brothel","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Derived from French 'bordel.' The default word for brothel in colloquial speech, journalism, and literature across the Spanish-speaking world. Neither highly clinical nor aggressively crude; occupies the mid-register between formal 'prostíbulo' and more colorful slang equivalents."}
{"term":"prostíbulo","lang":"es","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"sex_work_pornography","subcategory":"brothel","concept":"brothel","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"clinical","crude":18,"technical":68,"recognition_pct_current":88,"recognition_pct_peak":88,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Brothel; the formal and clinical Spanish term for an establishment providing paid sexual services; preferred in legal statutes, police reports, and official journalism over the colloquial 'burdel'.","english_term":"brothel","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Derived from Latin 'prostibulum' (place of prostitution, from 'prostare' = to stand in front of / to prostitute oneself). More formal in register than 'burdel'; standard in legal and institutional Spanish. Both terms are well understood across Latin America and Spain; 'prostíbulo' skews more formal and written."}
{"term":"lupanario","lang":"es","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":"brothel","concept":"brothel","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":12,"technical":78,"recognition_pct_current":18,"recognition_pct_peak":38,"peak_year":1800,"regional":null,"definition":"Brothel; a rare archaic and literary Spanish term derived from Latin 'lupanar' (brothel, from 'lupa' = she-wolf, Roman slang for a prostitute who howled to attract clients).","english_term":"lupanar","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"From Latin 'lupa' (she-wolf), used as Roman slang for a prostitute. 'Lupanar' is the classical Latin form; 'lupanario' is the Spanish nominal adaptation. Appears in scholarly translations of Roman texts, Golden Age literature, and historical writing. Not used in contemporary spoken Spanish; recognized mainly by classically educated speakers."}
